90:\begin{defn} Let A be an $n \times n$ real completely positive   matrix. The minimal $m$ such that $A = BB^{T}$ for some nonnegative $n \times m$  real matrix $B$,  is called the \textit{CP-rank} of $A$. The CP-rank of A is denoted by CP-rank$(A)$.
96:It would be of great interest to have an efficient algorithm to decide if a given matrix is completely positive or an efficient algorithm for computing the CP-rank of a given completely positive matrix.  While there is no efficient way of solving either problem for real matrices, we will see   that for special inclines there is an easy test for complete positivity.
99:\begin{conjecture}  \cite{DJL} If $A$ is a real CP-matrix of order $n \geq 4$ then CP-rank($A$) $\leq \left[n^{2}/4\right]$. \end{conjecture}
